From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: sunxi-ng: a733: the GPU's PLL, module clock, bus gates and reset

The GPU is the last compute block on this SoC with nothing in our CCU. PLL_GPU0,
GPU0_CLK_REG and GPU0_GATING_REG keep whatever the boot chain left in them and
no consumer can claim them. This patch adds six clocks and one reset and does
nothing else -- nothing in the tree references any of them until the device tree
in `arm64-dts-a733-add-the-gpu-node-disabled` does, and that node lands disabled.

This patch has to be applied after these:

  `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-gmac0-clocks` `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-ths-and-gpadc-clocks` `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-pwm0-pwm1-gates-and-resets` `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-edp-bus-gate-and-reset` `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-video-plls-and-edp-tv-pixel-clock` `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-display-engine-clock-gate-reset` `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-npu-pll-module-clock-and-resets`

-- because all of them append to the same three places this one does: the tail of
the clock id header, the tail of the reset id header, and the end of
sun60iw2_hw_clks including its .num line. Inserting six ids and six array entries
ahead of them moves the context every one of those hunks was generated against.
`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-gmac0-clocks` is hardware-proven GMAC0 and `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-npu-pll-module-clock-and-resets` is the NPU; neither may be regenerated to
make a number prettier. Measured 2026-08-27 with the series applied to
a 6.18.36 source tree up to the GPU patches, this patch applied, then the remainder attempted.

WHAT THE HARDWARE IS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Imagination PowerVR B-Series BXM-4-64 MC1, core window
0x01800000-0x0188FFFF, 576K, group GPU_SYS. Six clocks and one
reset, each naming one register field, every offset cross-checked against
the A733 user manual (UM v0.92):

  pll-gpu0    PLL_GPU0_CTRL_REG 0x00E0
  gpupll      the same register's single output tap, [22:20] P0 + bit 27 gate
  gpu0        GPU0_CLK_REG     0x0B20
  bus-gpu0    GPU0_GATING_REG  0x0B24 bit 0
  ahb-gpu0    AHB_MAT_CLK_GATING_REG  0x05C0 bit 7
  mbus-gpu0   MBUS_MAT_CLK_GATING_REG 0x05E0 bit 16
  RST_BUS_GPU0  GPU0_GATING_REG 0x0B24 bit 16, 0 = Assert, 1 = De-assert

THE THREE THINGS THAT MAKE THIS BLOCK DIFFERENT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. THE DIVIDER MASKS, IT DOES NOT DIVIDE. For the GPU clock, FACTOR_M counts
   how many of every 16 cycles are masked: GPU_CLK = CLK_SRC*((16-M)/16)
   (UM 4.1.4.4). The UM contrasts this with the other working clocks, which
   divide by counting cycles, so the exception is the UM's own statement,
   not a reading.
   Only M = 0, 8, 12, 14, 15 land on an integer ratio (/1 /2 /4 /8 /16); those
   five are the clk_div_table. A ccu_div without a table, or a ccu_mp, would
   read the field as a linear or power-of-two divisor and be wrong everywhere
   but at M = 0.

2. GPU0_UPD (bit 27) IS MANDATORY AND ITS ABSENCE IS INVISIBLE. GPU0 is one of
   the four clocks -- with NSI, MBUS and DRAM0 -- whose SEL, DIV1 and GATING
   fields take effect only after an explicit update; until the update is
   triggered, a readback returns the old parameters, not the new ones
   (UM 4.1.4.5). A driver that forgets it writes a rate that
   neither takes effect nor appears in a register dump. Mainline already has the
   mechanism: CCU_FEATURE_UPDATE_BIT with CCU_SUNXI_UPDATE_BIT = BIT(27)
   (ccu_common.h), honoured by ccu_div, ccu_mux and ccu_gate alike, and used by
   ccu-sun55i-a523.c for its mbus and dram clocks. This is its first use in our
   CCU.

3. THE UM CONSTRAINS THE ORDER, NOT ONLY THE VALUES. Going to a higher
   frequency, the divider is set first and the clock source second; going to
   a lower one, the reverse.
   CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT keeps the framework from crossing the
   mux on a rate request, so a rate change moves the divider alone and the rule
   cannot be broken by accident; which source drives the GPU stays a device-tree
   decision. The mux itself is glitch-free by the UM's own note,
   and all six of its sources -- GPUPLL, PERI0_800M/600M/400M/300M/200M -- are
   clocks this CCU already models, so no .index = -1 placeholder is needed.

ONE MODELLED DECISION, NAMED AS SUCH
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
bus-gpu0's parent is ahb-gpu0, not ahb. The GPU binding names at most three
clocks (core, mem, sys) and this block has four gates; an unclaimed gate is not
harmless, because it reads 1 at reset, nothing references it, and
clk_disable_unused() switches it off at late_initcall -- leaving the GPU without
its AHB port on the bus matrix. Chaining makes the dependency expressible. The
order is the plausible one (the block's gate behind its matrix port) but the UM
tabulates the two registers separately and states no relationship, so this is
modelled topology and the header says so. Falsifier, board work: with the chain
in place 0x05C0 bit 7 must read 1 whenever the GPU is bound and resumed; a
working GPU seen with that bit clear refutes the chain, and the fix is then a
fourth clock carrying CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED.

THE PLL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Fvco 1.26G-2.52G, 1/2 pre-divider, P0 1~8 (UM Table 4-2, PLL_GPU0 row).
The floor modelled here is 1272 MHz because the VCO is 24 MHz * N
and 1272 is the first multiple of 24 at or above the datasheet minimum -- the
same arithmetic `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-npu-pll-module-clock-and-resets` used for PLL_NPU. N = PLL_N + 1 with a UM minimum of 11 for
the PLL_N field, so N >= 12; M1 is the one-bit PLL_INPUT_DIV.
Reset value 0x4010_4500 decodes to N 70, P0 2 -> 840 MHz, which is what a GPU
left at reset runs at; the vendor operating point is 1008 MHz (N 84, and the
vendor's own OPP table pairs it with 960 mV, allwinner-bsp
configs/linux-6.6/sun60iw2p1.dtsi gpu-opp-table). Choosing a rate belongs to the
consumer, so this patch sets none.

PLL_GPU0_LOCK_CTRL 0x00E4, PAT0 0x00E8 and PAT1 0x00EC (CCU register list of the A733 user manual; decimal offsets 228/232/236)
are not modelled: they are the lock window and the spread-spectrum/fractional
pattern controls, nothing here needs a fractional rate, and the same three are
unmodelled for PLL_NPU. Named so the absence reads as a decision.

VERIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- applies: patch -p1 --fuzz=0 clean in the full ordered series on a freshly
  unpacked 6.18.36 (local, 2026-08-27).
- builds: a compile check passed, ccu-sun60iw2.o warning-free.
- the OBJECT was inspected, not the exit code, and the sizes were predicted
  before they were read: sun60iw2_ccu_clks 0x248 = 584 B = 73 entries (was 67),
  sun60iw2_hw_clks 0x2b8 = 696 B = 8 + 86*8 so .num really is CLK_MBUS_GPU0 + 1,
  sun60iw2_ccu_resets 0x100 = 256 B = 32 entries (was 31). All six clocks and the
  reset are in the maps, not only in the headers -- the RST_BUS_GMAC0
  silent-no-op trap.
- a binding-id consistency check passed over all 8 A733 headers; clock ids 80-85 and
  reset id 31 are the next free ones on main.
- ⚠ NOT hardware-proven. No register on a live GPU has been read for this patch,
  and `arm64-dts-a733-add-the-gpu-node-disabled` lands the consumer disabled for two separate reasons of its own.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/include/dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-ccu.h
+++ b/include/dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-ccu.h
@@ -125,4 +125,21 @@
 #define CLK_AHB_NPU		78
 #define CLK_MBUS_NPU		79
 
+/*
+ * The GPU. Six clocks, each naming one register field: PLL_GPU0's VCO
+ * (PLL_GPU0_CTRL 0x00E0, N and M1) and its single output tap "GPUPLL"
+ * ([22:20] P0 + output gate bit 27), the module clock GPU0_CLK 0x0B20, the
+ * block's own bus gate GPU0_GATING 0x0B24 bit 0, and the two bus-matrix
+ * master gates the GPU needs as an AHB/MBUS master -- AHB_MAT_CLK_GATING
+ * 0x05C0 bit 7 and MBUS_MAT_CLK_GATING 0x05E0 bit 16.
+ *
+ * 80-85 are the next free ids on main; 74-79 are the NPU's, added by `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-npu-pll-module-clock-and-resets`.
+ */
+#define CLK_PLL_GPU0		80
+#define CLK_GPUPLL		81
+#define CLK_GPU0		82
+#define CLK_BUS_GPU0		83
+#define CLK_AHB_GPU0		84
+#define CLK_MBUS_GPU0		85
+
 #endif /* _DT_BINDINGS_CLK_SUN60I_A733_CCU_H_ */
--- a/include/dt-bindings/reset/sun60i-a733-ccu.h
+++ b/include/dt-bindings/reset/sun60i-a733-ccu.h
@@ -58,4 +58,12 @@
 #define RST_BUS_NPU_AXI		29
 #define RST_BUS_NPU_AHB		30
 
+/*
+ * GPU -- GPU0_GATING_REG 0x0B24 bit16 (0 = Assert, 1 = De-assert). The
+ * register's other modelled bit is the bus gate at bit 0, which is a clock,
+ * not a reset. 31 is the next free reset id on main;
+ * 28-30 are the NPU's.
+ */
+#define RST_BUS_GPU0		31
+
 #endif /* _DT_BINDINGS_RST_SUN60I_A733_CCU_H_ */
--- a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60iw2.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60iw2.c
@@ -1501,6 +1501,172 @@
  *                       DE-asserted, and no consumer asks for it.
  */
 
+/*
+ * The GPU: Imagination PowerVR B-Series BXM-4-64 MC1, core window
+ * 0x01800000-0x0188FFFF (576K). Six clocks and one reset, each
+ * naming one register field:
+ *
+ *   pll-gpu0    PLL_GPU0_CTRL 0x00E0 -- the VCO (N [15:8], M1 [1])
+ *   gpupll      the same register's single output tap, [22:20] P0 gated by
+ *               PLL_OUTPUT_GATE bit 27
+ *   gpu0        GPU0_CLK_REG 0x0B20 -- mux [26:24], divider [3:0], gate bit31
+ *   bus-gpu0    GPU0_GATING_REG 0x0B24 bit 0 -- the block's own bus gate
+ *   ahb-gpu0    AHB_MAT_CLK_GATING_REG  0x05C0 bit 7
+ *   mbus-gpu0   MBUS_MAT_CLK_GATING_REG 0x05E0 bit 16
+ *   RST_BUS_GPU0  GPU0_GATING_REG 0x0B24 bit 16 (0 = Assert, 1 = De-assert)
+ *
+ * ⚠ THE DIVIDER DOES NOT DIVIDE, IT MASKS. The UM says so in its own section
+ * rather than leaving it to be inferred: for the GPU clock, FACTOR_M counts
+ * how many of every 16 cycles are masked, GPU_CLK =
+ * CLK_SRC*((16-M)/16) (4.1.4.4), unlike the other working clocks, which
+ * divide by counting cycles, as the UM says one
+ * sentence earlier. Only five of the sixteen field values land on an integer
+ * ratio -- M = 0, 8, 12, 14, 15 give /1, /2, /4, /8, /16 -- and those five are
+ * the table below. A plain ccu_div or ccu_mp would read the field as a linear
+ * or power-of-two divisor and be wrong at every value but 0.
+ *
+ * ⚠ GPU0_UPD (bit 27) IS NOT OPTIONAL, AND ITS ABSENCE IS INVISIBLE. GPU0 is
+ * one of the four clocks (with NSI, MBUS and DRAM0) whose SEL, DIV1 and
+ * GATING fields need an explicit update: write 1 to the GPU0_UPD bit to
+ * latch the configured parameters; until then, a readback returns the
+ * original state, and after it the updated parameters
+ * (4.1.4.5). Without it a rate write neither takes effect
+ * nor shows up in a register dump -- it reads back as the old value, which is
+ * the failure mode that looks like "the clock is already right".
+ * CCU_FEATURE_UPDATE_BIT is mainline's name for exactly this, and
+ * CCU_SUNXI_UPDATE_BIT is BIT(27) (ccu_common.h); ccu_div, ccu_mux and
+ * ccu_gate all set it when the feature is on, so the whole clock is covered
+ * and not only its rate.
+ *
+ * ⚠ The UM's ordering rule is a constraint on any consumer that changes both
+ * halves at once: going up in frequency, program the divider
+ * before the clock source; going down, do it the other way round,
+ * source first and divider second. Changing only the divider, as done
+ * here, never violates it.
+ * CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT keeps the framework from wandering
+ * across the mux on a rate request, so a rate change moves the divider alone
+ * and the rule cannot be broken by accident; which source drives the GPU is a
+ * device-tree decision. The same rule's other half -- the reset is
+ * released first and the gating clock is opened afterwards
+ * -- belongs to the consumer's probe order, not here.
+ *
+ * The mux's six sources are the UM's. All six are
+ * modelled: ccu_mux_helper_get_parent() hands the raw field back as an index,
+ * so a short array does not fail loudly, it mis-names or (at 110/111, which
+ * the UM leaves undocumented for a 3-bit field) returns a parentless clock.
+ * Every one of the six is a clock this CCU already has, which is why none of
+ * them needs the .index = -1 placeholder the NPU mux uses for the VE PLLs.
+ *
+ * ⚠ ahb-gpu0 and mbus-gpu0 share their REGISTERS with the SERDES gates above
+ * and with the NPU's, one bit apart; they are separate clocks, not aliases.
+ * Both read 1 at reset (GPU0_AHB_GATE_SW_CFG bit 7 default
+ * 0x1, GPU0_MBUS_GATE_SW_CFG bit 16 default 0x1), and the NPU's pair was
+ * measured CLEAR on a vendor-kernel board with its block runtime-suspended -- so they
+ * are per-use software gates, not a documentation artefact. Modelled for the
+ * same reason: a master that cannot reach the bus matrix is a GPU that faults
+ * on its first fetch. Parented to ahb because this driver does not model the
+ * MBUS root; that is a topology stand-in, not a rate claim -- neither gate
+ * divides anything.
+ *
+ * ⚠ PLL_GPU0 has three more registers this driver does not model:
+ * PLL_GPU0_LOCK_CTRL 0x00E4, PAT0 0x00E8 and PAT1 0x00EC (the CCU register list of the A733 user manual offsets
+ * 228/232/236). They are the lock-window and the spread-spectrum/fractional
+ * pattern controls. Nothing here needs a fractional rate -- every operating
+ * point the vendor uses is an integer multiple of 24 MHz -- and the same
+ * three are unmodelled for PLL_NPU. Named so their absence reads as a
+ * decision.
+ *
+ * Fvco 1.26G-2.52G with a 1/2 pre-divider and P0 1~8 (UM Table 4-2, PLL_GPU0
+ * row). The floor here is 1272 MHz, not 1260: the VCO is
+ * 24 MHz * N and 1272 is the first multiple of 24 at or above the datasheet
+ * minimum. N = PLL_N + 1 with a UM minimum of 11 for PLL_N,
+ * so N >= 12. Reset value 0x4010_4500 = N 70, P0 2 -> 840 MHz, which
+ * is the rate a GPU left at reset would run; the vendor operating point is
+ * 1008 MHz (N 84), and choosing it belongs to the consumer's device tree.
+ */
+#define SUN60IW2_PLL_GPU0_REG		0x00E0
+#define SUN60IW2_PLL_GPU0_MIN_VCO	1272000000U
+#define SUN60IW2_PLL_GPU0_MAX_VCO	2520000000U
+
+static struct ccu_nm pll_gpu0_clk = {
+	.enable		= BIT(31),
+	.lock		= BIT(28),
+	.n		= _SUNXI_CCU_MULT_OFFSET_MIN_MAX(8, 8, 1, 12, 255),
+	.m		= _SUNXI_CCU_DIV(1, 1),
+	.min_rate	= SUN60IW2_PLL_GPU0_MIN_VCO,
+	.max_rate	= SUN60IW2_PLL_GPU0_MAX_VCO,
+	.common		= {
+		.reg		= SUN60IW2_PLL_GPU0_REG,
+		.hw.init	= CLK_HW_INIT_PARENTS_HW("pll-gpu0",
+				pll_ref_hws, &ccu_nm_ops,
+				CLK_SET_RATE_UNGATE),
+	},
+};
+
+static const struct clk_hw *pll_gpu0_hws[] = { &pll_gpu0_clk.common.hw };
+
+static SUNXI_CCU_M_HWS_WITH_GATE(gpupll_clk, "gpupll", pll_gpu0_hws,
+				 SUN60IW2_PLL_GPU0_REG, 20, 3, BIT(27),
+				 CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT);
+
+static const struct clk_parent_data gpu0_parents[] = {
+	{ .hw = &gpupll_clk.common.hw },		/* 000 GPUPLL     */
+	{ .hw = &pll_peri0_800m_clk.common.hw },	/* 001 PERI0_800M */
+	{ .hw = &pll_peri0_600m_clk.hw },		/* 010 PERI0_600M */
+	{ .hw = &pll_peri0_400m_clk.hw },		/* 011 PERI0_400M */
+	{ .hw = &pll_peri0_300m_clk.hw },		/* 100 PERI0_300M */
+	{ .hw = &pll_peri0_200m_clk.hw },		/* 101 PERI0_200M */
+};
+
+/* GPU0_DIV1 [3:0]: masked cycles per 16, so the ratio is 16/(16-M). */
+static struct clk_div_table gpu0_div_table[] = {
+	{ .val = 0,  .div = 1  },
+	{ .val = 8,  .div = 2  },
+	{ .val = 12, .div = 4  },
+	{ .val = 14, .div = 8  },
+	{ .val = 15, .div = 16 },
+	{ /* sentinel */ }
+};
+
+static struct ccu_div gpu0_clk = {
+	.enable		= BIT(31),
+	.div		= _SUNXI_CCU_DIV_TABLE(0, 4, gpu0_div_table),
+	.mux		= _SUNXI_CCU_MUX(24, 3),
+	.common		= {
+		.reg		= 0x0B20,
+		.features	= CCU_FEATURE_UPDATE_BIT,
+		.hw.init	= CLK_HW_INIT_PARENTS_DATA("gpu0",
+				gpu0_parents, &ccu_div_ops,
+				CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT |
+				CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT),
+	},
+};
+
+static SUNXI_CCU_GATE_HWS(ahb_gpu0_clk,  "ahb-gpu0",  ahb_hws, 0x05C0, BIT(7),  0);
+
+static const struct clk_hw *ahb_gpu0_hws[] = { &ahb_gpu0_clk.common.hw };
+
+/*
+ * ⚠ bus-gpu0 hangs off ahb-gpu0 AND THAT PARENT IS A DECISION, not a reading.
+ * The binding this GPU uses names at most three clocks -- core, mem, sys
+ * (Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpu/img,powervr-rogue.yaml) -- and this
+ * block has four gates. An unclaimed gate is not harmless: it reads 1 at
+ * reset, nothing takes a reference on it, and clk_disable_unused() turns it
+ * off at late_initcall, which would leave the GPU without its AHB port on the
+ * bus matrix. Chaining makes the dependency expressible: the device tree
+ * claims bus-gpu0 as "sys", and enabling it enables the matrix port with it.
+ * The order is the plausible one -- the block's own gate sits behind its port
+ * -- but the UM tabulates the two registers separately and states no
+ * relationship between them, so this is modelled topology.
+ *
+ * Falsifier, and it needs a board: with the chain in place 0x05C0 bit 7 must
+ * read 1 whenever the GPU is bound and resumed. If a GPU that works is ever
+ * seen with that bit clear, the chain is wrong and the fix is a fourth clock
+ * with CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED, not a rate or a reset change.
+ */
+static SUNXI_CCU_GATE_HWS(bus_gpu0_clk,  "bus-gpu0",  ahb_gpu0_hws, 0x0B24, BIT(0), 0);
+static SUNXI_CCU_GATE_HWS(mbus_gpu0_clk, "mbus-gpu0", ahb_hws, 0x05E0, BIT(16), 0);
+
 /* ccu_common-based clocks (registered and reset-mapped by the core). */
 static struct ccu_common *sun60iw2_ccu_clks[] = {
 	&pll_ref_clk.common,
@@ -1570,6 +1736,12 @@
 	&bus_npu_clk.common,
 	&ahb_npu_clk.common,
 	&mbus_npu_clk.common,
+	&pll_gpu0_clk.common,
+	&gpupll_clk.common,
+	&gpu0_clk.common,
+	&ahb_gpu0_clk.common,
+	&bus_gpu0_clk.common,
+	&mbus_gpu0_clk.common,
 };
 
 static struct clk_hw_onecell_data sun60iw2_hw_clks = {
@@ -1654,8 +1826,14 @@
 		[CLK_BUS_NPU]		= &bus_npu_clk.common.hw,
 		[CLK_AHB_NPU]		= &ahb_npu_clk.common.hw,
 		[CLK_MBUS_NPU]		= &mbus_npu_clk.common.hw,
+		[CLK_PLL_GPU0]		= &pll_gpu0_clk.common.hw,
+		[CLK_GPUPLL]		= &gpupll_clk.common.hw,
+		[CLK_GPU0]		= &gpu0_clk.common.hw,
+		[CLK_BUS_GPU0]		= &bus_gpu0_clk.common.hw,
+		[CLK_AHB_GPU0]		= &ahb_gpu0_clk.common.hw,
+		[CLK_MBUS_GPU0]		= &mbus_gpu0_clk.common.hw,
 	},
-	.num	= CLK_MBUS_NPU + 1,
+	.num	= CLK_MBUS_GPU0 + 1,
 };
 
 static struct ccu_reset_map sun60iw2_ccu_resets[] = {
@@ -1690,6 +1868,7 @@
 	[RST_BUS_NPU]		= { 0x0B04, BIT(16) },
 	[RST_BUS_NPU_AXI]	= { 0x0B04, BIT(17) },
 	[RST_BUS_NPU_AHB]	= { 0x0B04, BIT(18) },
+	[RST_BUS_GPU0]		= { 0x0B24, BIT(16) },
 };
 
 static const struct sunxi_ccu_desc sun60iw2_ccu_desc = {
